The 10-K says legislative and regulatory initiatives outside the U.S. could force JPMorgan to make significant changes to its operations and legal entity structure in the countries or regions involved in order to comply. The rules it cites cover:
- setting up locally-based intermediate holding companies or operating subsidiaries;
- keeping minimum capital or liquidity in local subsidiaries;
- ring-fencing core banking from markets activities;
- resolution regimes;
- requirements to execute or settle trades on exchanges or through central counterparties;
- position limits and reporting rules for derivatives;
- governance and accountability regimes;
- conduct of business and control requirements;
- limits on compensation.

The filing says such differences in regulation have required, and could again require, the Firm to:
- divest assets or restructure;
- absorb higher operational, capital and liquidity costs;
- change its prices;
- curtail products, services or other business opportunities;
- pay more to comply with different legal frameworks.

The 2022 10-K does not mention First Republic. The 2023 10-K adds a new risk factor saying the acquisition, completed May 1, 2023 from the FDIC, may not deliver all the expected benefits. Results could fall short on the final purchase price settlement, the total cost and time of integration, the performance of the acquired assets and liabilities, or an improved JPMorgan stock price. It also warns of several other risks:
- integration could disrupt ongoing businesses or create inconsistencies in controls and policies that hurt client relationships;
- key employees could leave;
- unexpected costs or losses could arise, including if JPMorgan fails to meet the conditions of its shared-loss agreements with the FDIC, which could reduce the credit-loss coverage those agreements provide.

The summary of operational risks was also updated to include acquisition risks, naming First Republic.

In 2021 Ford framed EV risk mostly as a hypothetical: if the EV market didn't develop at the expected rate, even with regulation encouraging rapid adoption, or if EVs were viewed negatively or consumers preferred rivals, results could suffer. By 2024 the risk was described as already happening. Ford said it had seen lower-than-anticipated industrywide EV adoption, that regulation was shifting away from supporting rapid adoption, and that it had adjusted spending, production and launches as a result. The 2024 filing also adds several new EV risks:
- near-term EV pricing pressure and high battery costs;
- charges tied to EV suppliers and the risk of not recouping battery-plant investments;
- dependence on battery raw materials (lithium, cobalt, nickel) and offtake contracts that leave the volume risk with Ford;
- competition from Chinese EV makers;
- the possible repeal or restriction of IRA EV incentives.
